WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.429 --> 00:00:04.499
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.499 --> 00:00:10.530
In this series, we frequently talk about Generative
Adversarial networks, or GANs in short.

00:00:10.530 --> 00:00:16.379
This means a pair of neural networks that
battle each over time to master a task, for

00:00:16.379 --> 00:00:21.240
instance, to generate realistic looking images
from a written description.

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:26.680
Here you see NVIDIA’s amazing work that
was able to dream up high-resolution images

00:00:26.680 --> 00:00:29.080
of imaginary celebrities.

00:00:29.080 --> 00:00:33.790
In the next episode, we will talk some more
about their newest work that does something

00:00:33.790 --> 00:00:37.590
like this, and is even better at it, believe
it or not.

00:00:37.590 --> 00:00:42.290
I hope you have subscribed to the channel
to make sure not to miss out on that one.

00:00:42.290 --> 00:00:48.370
And for now, while we marvel at these outstanding
results, I will quickly tell you about overfitting

00:00:48.370 --> 00:00:51.880
and what it has to do with images of celebrities.

00:00:51.880 --> 00:00:56.829
When we train a neural network, we wish to
make sure that it understands the concepts

00:00:56.829 --> 00:00:58.460
we are trying to teach it.

00:00:58.460 --> 00:01:04.150
Typically, we feed it a database of labeled
images, where the labels mean that this depicts

00:01:04.150 --> 00:01:07.680
a dog, and this one is not a dog, but a cat.

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:13.410
After the training step took place, in the
ideal case it will be able to build an understanding

00:01:13.410 --> 00:01:18.170
of these images, so that when we show them
new, previously unseen pictures, it would

00:01:18.170 --> 00:01:22.270
be able to correctly guess which animals they
depict.

00:01:22.270 --> 00:01:27.910
However, in many cases, we start training
the neural network, and during the training,

00:01:27.910 --> 00:01:33.480
it gives us wonderful results, and it gets
the animals right every single time!

00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:40.210
But, whenever it sees new, previously unseen
images, it can’t tell a dog from a cat at

00:01:40.210 --> 00:01:41.210
all.

00:01:41.210 --> 00:01:47.320
This peculiar case is what we call overfitting,
and this is the bane of machine learning research.

00:01:47.320 --> 00:01:52.020
Overfitting is like the kind of student we
all encountered at school who is always very

00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:57.409
good at memorizing the textbook but can’t
solve even the simplest new problems on the

00:01:57.409 --> 00:01:58.590
exam.

00:01:58.590 --> 00:02:02.320
This is not learning, this is memorization!

00:02:02.320 --> 00:02:06.860
Overfitting means that a neural network does
not learn the concept of dogs or cats, it

00:02:06.860 --> 00:02:12.519
just tries to memorize this database of images
and is able to regurgitate it for us, but

00:02:12.519 --> 00:02:15.760
its knowledge cannot generalize for new images.

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:17.349
That’s not good.

00:02:17.349 --> 00:02:20.849
I want intelligence, not a copying machine!

00:02:20.849 --> 00:02:26.470
So at this point, it is probably clearer what
images of celebrities have to do with overfitting.

00:02:26.470 --> 00:02:32.409
So, how do we know that this algorithm doesn’t
just memorize the celebrity image dataset

00:02:32.409 --> 00:02:37.069
it was given, and can really generate new,
imaginary people?

00:02:37.069 --> 00:02:41.340
Is it the good kind of student, or the lousy
student?

00:02:41.340 --> 00:02:47.209
Technique number one, let’s not just dream
up images of new celebrities, but also visualize

00:02:47.209 --> 00:02:50.829
images from the training data that are similar
to this image.

00:02:50.829 --> 00:02:54.319
If they are too similar, we have an overfitting
problem.

00:02:54.319 --> 00:02:55.810
Let’s have a look.

00:02:55.810 --> 00:03:01.480
Now it is easy to see that this is proper
intelligence, and not a copying machine, because

00:03:01.480 --> 00:03:07.769
it was clearly able to learn the facial features
of these people and combine them in novel

00:03:07.769 --> 00:03:08.769
ways.

00:03:08.769 --> 00:03:14.260
This is what scientists at NVIDIA did in their
paper and are to be commended for that.

00:03:14.260 --> 00:03:20.189
Technique number two, well, just take a bunch
of humans and let them decide whether these

00:03:20.189 --> 00:03:25.280
images differ from the training set and if
they are realistic.

00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:31.459
This kinda works, but of course, costs quite
a bit of money, labor, and we end up with

00:03:31.459 --> 00:03:33.269
something subjective.

00:03:33.269 --> 00:03:38.290
We better not compare the quality of research
papers based on that if we can avoid it.

00:03:38.290 --> 00:03:44.790
And get this, we can actually avoid it by
using something called the inception score.

00:03:44.790 --> 00:03:50.139
Instead of using humans, this score uses a
neural network to have a look at these images

00:03:50.139 --> 00:03:54.530
and measure the quality and the diversity
of the results.

00:03:54.530 --> 00:03:59.680
As long as the image produces similar neuron
activations within this neural network, two

00:03:59.680 --> 00:04:02.129
images will be deemed to be similar.

00:04:02.129 --> 00:04:07.199
Finally, this score is an objective way of
measuring progress within this field, and

00:04:07.199 --> 00:04:09.559
it is of course, subject to maximization.

00:04:09.559 --> 00:04:15.459
So now, you, of course, wish to know what
the state of the art is today.

00:04:15.459 --> 00:04:21.989
For reference, a set of real images has an
inception score of 233, and the best works

00:04:21.989 --> 00:04:28.120
that produced synthetic images from just a
few years ago had a score of around 50.

00:04:28.120 --> 00:04:33.069
To the best of my knowledge, as of the publishing
of this video, the highest inception score

00:04:33.069 --> 00:04:39.389
for an AI is close to 166, so we’ve come
a long long way.

00:04:39.389 --> 00:04:41.990
You can see some of these images here.

00:04:41.990 --> 00:04:45.870
Truly exciting — what a time to be alive!

00:04:45.870 --> 00:04:51.120
The disadvantages of the method is that one,
because the diversity of the outputs is also

00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:55.509
to be measured, it requires many thousands
of images.

00:04:55.509 --> 00:05:01.090
This is likely more of an issue with the problem
definition itself, not this method, and also,

00:05:01.090 --> 00:05:05.699
since this means that the computers and not
real people have to do more work, … we can

00:05:05.699 --> 00:05:07.710
give this one a pass.

00:05:07.710 --> 00:05:11.789
Disadvantage number two, I will include this
paper in the video description for you, which

00:05:11.789 --> 00:05:17.449
basically describes that there are cases where
it is possible to get the network to think

00:05:17.449 --> 00:05:22.800
an image is of higher quality than another
one even if it clearly isn’t.

00:05:22.800 --> 00:05:27.540
Now you see that we have pretty ingenious
techniques to measure the quality of image

00:05:27.540 --> 00:05:33.219
generator AI programs, and of course, this
area of research is also subject to improvement,

00:05:33.219 --> 00:05:36.169
and, I’ll be here to tell you about it.

00:05:36.169 --> 00:05:40.200
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

